  "reasoning": "The claim that monosodium glutamate (MSG) causes headaches, numbness, and related symptoms (historically termed \"Chinese restaurant syndrome\") has been extensively debunked by scientific research. Decades of double-blind, placebo-controlled studies have failed to demonstrate a consistent, reproducible causal link between MSG consumed in normal dietary amounts and these symptoms. Major global food safety and health authorities—including the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA), the World Health Organization (WHO), and the European Food Safety Authority (EFSA)—have classified MSG as \"Generally Recognized as Safe\" (GRAS). Furthermore, chemically, the body metabolizes added glutamate in MSG in the exact same manner as naturally occurring glutamate found abundantly in foods like tomatoes, mushrooms, and aged cheeses.",
